     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Sure! The Shapiro-Wilk test is a way to check if your data looks like it comes from a normal distribution (which is a bell-shaped curve). This is important because many statistical tests work best when the data follows this normal pattern.\n",
      "\n",
      "Here’s how you can think about it in simple terms:\n",
      "\n",
      "1. **Normal Distribution**: Imagine you have a bunch of numbers that, if plotted on a graph, would form a nice, symmetrical hill shape (that's what a normal distribution looks like).\n",
      "\n",
      "2. **Checking Your Data**: When you do experiments or surveys, sometimes your results don't naturally fall into this perfect bell curve. You might want to know if they are close enough for most statistical tests to still give reliable results.\n",
      "\n",
      "3. **The Test**: The Shapiro-Wilk test helps you determine if your data is \"normal enough.\" It does this by comparing the distribution of your data to what a normal distribution would look like. \n",
      "\n",
      "4. **How it Works**:\n",
      "   - It calculates a statistic (let's call it W) that compares how well your data fits the expected pattern.\n",
      "   - Then, it checks if this value is close enough to 1, which means your data likely comes from a normal distribution.\n",
      "\n",
      "5. **Results**: If the test gives you a result where the p-value is greater than 0.05 (or some other threshold), you can say that your data probably follows a normal distribution. Otherwise, it might be better to use methods for non-normal data.\n",
      "\n",
      "In essence, it’s like checking if your data fits into the ideal \"bell\" shape before applying certain statistical tests, ensuring more accurate results.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
